On Jan 27, 9:54=A0pm, zana...@gmail.com wrote:
> Imho orcish wizard as an idea is cool. How does D&D, or, say,
> Warhammer deal with orcish magic users - are they impossible
> or improbable? They dabble with dark magic (which is cool), right?

D&D certainly allows orcish wizards, although they aren't very good
at it. It works much the same way as in NetHack, really, except
that you have to put your high scores into Intelligence just to get a
decent Intelligence value for your spellcasting. A D&D orcish wizard
would likely have a higher strength then the typical D&D wizard
(probably about 12 or so even if the stat was 'dumped', and
potentially very high if the player used it as their second-highest
stat
after Intelligence) and a ridiculously low Charisma.

As for the sort of magic they used, that would be up to the wizard,
but yes, the entire spectrum of evil-descriptor spells would be open
to the wizard in question (assuming a typical evil orc).

A typical human wizard in D&D might be Str 8, Dex 13, Con 14,
Int 15, Wis 10, Cha 12; the orcish wizard would look like Str 12,
Dex 13, Con 14, Int 13, Wis 8, Cha 10 with the same statline, but
a sensible orcish wizard who wanted to take advantage of his or
her strength would probably rearrange that to Str 16, Dex 12,
Con 13, Int 13, Wis 6, Cha 8; an Intelligence of 13 is not good for
a wizard, but it's enough (at least for lower-level spells). This
orc's
likely to need magical aid to cast the more powerful spells available,
though. So I'd say not impossible, but somewhat improbable; that
orc's going to be bottom of wizard class (but quite capable of
punching his teacher into a pulp).

